    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d702">

                <pb facs="0957" n="843" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg843"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.415 Traders/30</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Under Secretary of State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Castle</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Counselor of the British Embassy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Osborne</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-12-01">December 1, 1931</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Osborne</hi>: I have received your letter
                    of November 17, on the subject of entry privileges for Australian business men
                    desiring to come to the United States to carry on international trade.</p>
                <p>The Department plans to bring this question to the attention of the appropriate
                    committee of Congress as soon as practicable after Congress reconvenes in
                    December. It is hoped that in the near future it will be possible to indicate to
                    you what steps can be taken to effect a satisfactory adjustment of the
                    matter.</p>
                <p>Very sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">W. R. Castle, Jr.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
